  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nContinued WAA and a moist airmass moving in from the west will make \nfor a hot and humid Sunday. Highs will be in the upper 80s to near \n90 degrees with dewpoints in the low to mid 70s. Mid level stratus \nare expected to persist through at least daybreak this morning, with \nskies gradually clearing by the afternoon. Skies may appear hazy at \ntimes as smoke from ongoing wildfires in the Pacific Northwest \ndrifts in but remains elevated. No impacts to air quality or \nvisibility are expected. \n\nThe hot and humid conditions Sunday and Monday will fuel an unstable \nenvironment for storms around the periphery of a building upper \nlevel ridge over the central US. Severe weather is possible late \ntonight into early Monday morning, then again Monday afternoon \nthrough Monday night. 00z and 06z CAM guidance is still split on \nwhat exactly will occur, but the consensus is that timing is \ntrending slightly later on the first possible round of storms \ntonight. Some of the CAMs have storms entering our area as late as \ndaybreak Monday. We will have to monitor upstream convection this \nevening in Wisconsin; if an MCS forms, this complex is expected to \nmove southeast overnight into early Monday along the edge of the \nupper level ridge. Whether it maintains its strength into our area \nis uncertain, hence a broad Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for severe \nweather that has been maintained by SPC. If any storms do make it \ninto our area, damaging winds would be the main threat. \n\nBetter chances for severe weather exist on Monday with a Slight Risk \n(level 2 of 5) and potentially more favorable diurnal timing. The \nenvironment will be similar Monday with a very unstable airmass\noverhead due to the heat and humidity. Should there be storms \nin the area Monday morning, lingering cloud debris could \ncertainty delay the timing but for now thinking severe weather \npossible within a broad window of 2 PM EDT Monday to 2 AM EDT \nTuesday. With up to 3000- 4000 J/kg SBCAPE, 30-50 kts of shear, \nand 7-8 C/km mid level lapse rates, storms are expected to grow \nupscale into a squall line or mature MCS and propagate \nsouth/southeast through our forecast area. Damaging winds will \nonce again be the main threat. A brief embedded tornado or two \nwill be possible as well as 0-1 SRH values approach 150-200 \nm2/s2 Monday evening into Monday night ahead of the line. \n\nTuesday and beyond will see a reprieve in the active weather pattern \nas high pressure builds over the Great Lakes. Dry and seasonable \nwith highs in the mid 80s midweek. Intermittent rain/storm chances \nby the end of the week. \n\nAdditionally, as winds shift to become northwesterly on Tuesday, \ndangerous swimming conditions are expected to develop for southeast \nLake Michigan beaches on Tuesday. Waves of 3 to 5 feet Tuesday \nmorning will build to 7 to 10 feet by Tuesday afternoon/evening. \nWith direct onshore flow, strong structural, rip, and longshore \ncurrents are expected. Structural currents will be especially strong \non the north side of piers. Stay out of the water and stay off of \nthe piers! Beach Hazards Statements will likely be needed in the \ncoming days for La Porte County, IN and Berrien County, MI.",
